### What triggers a rebalance?

A rebalance happens whenever group membership or topic metadata changes: a consumer joins, a consumer leaves or crashes, or partitions are added. The protocol decides how disruptive that reassignment is.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

public class RebalanceTriggers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Rebalance triggers:");
        System.out.println("  1. Consumer joins the group (new instance or restart)");
        System.out.println("  2. Consumer leaves (shutdown or crash)");
        System.out.println("  3. Consumer misses heartbeat (session timeout)");
        System.out.println("  4. Consumer exceeds max.poll.interval.ms");
        System.out.println("  5. Topic partition count changes");
        System.out.println("  6. Topic is created/deleted matching subscription");
    }
}
```

```text
Rebalance triggers:
  1. Consumer joins the group (new instance or restart)
  2. Consumer leaves (shutdown or crash)
  3. Consumer misses heartbeat (session timeout)
  4. Consumer exceeds max.poll.interval.ms
  5. Topic partition count changes
  6. Topic is created/deleted matching subscription
```

### How does the eager protocol work?

The eager protocol works in one phase. Every consumer first revokes all of its currently owned partitions, which means it stops consuming and commits offsets. Then the group leader computes a brand-new assignment and every consumer picks up its newly assigned partitions. Even a consumer whose partitions do not change still gives them up and reacquires them.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import java.util.Properties;

public class EagerConfig {
    public static Properties eagerConsumer() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "settlement-service");
        props.put("key.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put("value.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        // Eager assignors -- stop-the-world rebalance
        props.put("partition.assignment.strategy",
            "org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.RangeAssignor");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Eager rebalance steps:");
        System.out.println("  1. ALL consumers revoke ALL partitions");
        System.out.println("  2. ALL consumers STOP processing");
        System.out.println("  3. Leader computes new assignment");
        System.out.println("  4. ALL consumers get new assignment");
        System.out.println("  5. ALL consumers resume");
        System.out.println("  Impact: entire group pauses");
    }
}
```

```text
Eager rebalance steps:
  1. ALL consumers revoke ALL partitions
  2. ALL consumers STOP processing
  3. Leader computes new assignment
  4. ALL consumers get new assignment
  5. ALL consumers resume
  Impact: entire group pauses
```

### How does the cooperative-sticky protocol work?

The cooperative-sticky protocol works incrementally over two short rebalances. On the first rebalance it computes the desired assignment and only revokes the partitions that need to move. Consumers that keep their partitions never stop. The second short rebalance assigns the freed partitions to their new owners.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import java.util.Properties;

public class CooperativeConfig {
    public static Properties cooperativeConsumer() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "settlement-service");
        props.put("key.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put("value.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        // Cooperative-sticky -- incremental rebalance
        props.put("partition.assignment.strategy",
            "org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.CooperativeStickyAssignor");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Cooperative rebalance steps:");
        System.out.println("  1. Compute desired assignment");
        System.out.println("  2. Only MOVING partitions are revoked");
        System.out.println("  3. Non-moving consumers KEEP processing");
        System.out.println("  4. Second short rebalance assigns freed partitions");
        System.out.println("  Impact: only migrating partitions pause");
    }
}
```

```text
Cooperative rebalance steps:
  1. Compute desired assignment
  2. Only MOVING partitions are revoked
  3. Non-moving consumers KEEP processing
  4. Second short rebalance assigns freed partitions
  Impact: only migrating partitions pause
```

### How do the two protocols compare? (comparison table)

| Feature | Eager | Cooperative-Sticky |
|---|---|---|
| Revocation scope | All partitions on all consumers | Only partitions that move |
| Processing pause | Entire group stops | Only migrating partitions pause |
| Number of rebalance rounds | 1 | 2 (short) |
| Assignment stability | New assignment from scratch | Sticky -- tries to keep same mapping |
| Suitable for rolling deploys | Poor (each restart stalls group) | Excellent (localized pause) |
| Assignor class | RangeAssignor, RoundRobinAssignor | CooperativeStickyAssignor |

### How do you migrate from eager to cooperative?

You cannot mix eager and cooperative assignors in the same group because they disagree on revocation. The migration is a two-phase rolling upgrade.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class RebalanceMigration {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Phase 1: Rolling deploy with BOTH assignors");
        System.out.println("  partition.assignment.strategy = "
            + "RangeAssignor, CooperativeStickyAssignor");
        System.out.println("  All consumers now support both protocols");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Phase 2: Rolling deploy with ONLY cooperative");
        System.out.println("  partition.assignment.strategy = "
            + "CooperativeStickyAssignor");
        System.out.println("  Migration complete");
    }
}
```

```text
Phase 1: Rolling deploy with BOTH assignors
  partition.assignment.strategy = RangeAssignor, CooperativeStickyAssignor
  All consumers now support both protocols

Phase 2: Rolling deploy with ONLY cooperative
  partition.assignment.strategy = CooperativeStickyAssignor
  Migration complete
```

### How do you handle offsets during rebalance?

Commit offsets in `onPartitionsRevoked` so no work is double-processed when partitions move to another consumer.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRebalanceListener;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.util.Collection;

public class OffsetCommitOnRebalance implements ConsumerRebalanceListener {
    private final KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer;

    public OffsetCommitOnRebalance(KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer) {
        this.consumer = consumer;
    }

    @Override
    public void onPartitionsRevoked(Collection<TopicPartition> partitions) {
        System.out.println("Partitions revoked: " + partitions);
        consumer.commitSync();
        System.out.println("Offsets committed before handoff");
    }

    @Override
    public void onPartitionsAssigned(Collection<TopicPartition> partitions) {
        System.out.println("Partitions assigned: " + partitions);
    }
}
```

### How does static group membership complement cooperative rebalancing?

Setting `group.instance.id` makes the consumer a static member. If it restarts and rejoins within the session timeout, no rebalance is triggered at all.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import java.util.Properties;

public class StaticMembershipConfig {
    public static Properties staticConsumer(String instanceId) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "settlement-service");
        props.put("group.instance.id", instanceId);
        props.put("session.timeout.ms", "45000");
        props.put("partition.assignment.strategy",
            "org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.CooperativeStickyAssignor");
        props.put("key.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put("value.deserializer",
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Static membership + cooperative:");
        System.out.println("  Quick restart within 45s -> no rebalance at all");
        System.out.println("  Long outage beyond 45s   -> cooperative rebalance");
    }
}
```

```text
Static membership + cooperative:
  Quick restart within 45s -> no rebalance at all
  Long outage beyond 45s   -> cooperative rebalance
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Cooperative-sticky dramatically reduces the impact of routine events like rolling deployments. In a bank running continuous deployments, the eager protocol makes the whole settlement group stall on every consumer restart, while cooperative-sticky confines the pause to just the partitions owned by the restarting consumer. The migration caution is that you cannot flip the whole group at once -- you must do a two-phase rolling upgrade. Static membership with a group instance id eliminates rebalance entirely for quick restarts.
